---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import { risks, riskGroups, riskLevels } from "../../data/risks";
import "../../styles/risks.css";
---
<BaseLayout title="Risks & Safety" description="Understand AI risks, what evidence supports them, how AI might change work, and what safeguards can help." bodyClass="exhibit-body">
  <SiteHeader subtle />
  <main id="content" class="risks-realm" data-risks-root>
    <section class="rs-hero" aria-labelledby="rs-title">
      <div class="rs-hero__field" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="rs-hero__inner">
        <div class="rs-hero__copy">
          <a href="/discover/" class="rs-back">← All perspectives</a>
          <p class="rs-eyebrow"><span class="rs-eyebrow__mark"></span> PERSPECTIVE 01 <span class="rs-divide">/</span> RISKS & SAFETY</p>
          <h1 id="rs-title">What should we<br/><em>really worry about?</em></h1>
          <p class="rs-hero__lead">There's no shortage of scary headlines about AI. Some describe real harm. Others warn about things that might happen later. Let's look at the difference — and what we can do about it.</p>
          <div class="rs-hero__actions"><a href="#risk-radar" class="rs-primary">Explore the risks <span aria-hidden="true">↘</span></a><a href="#risk-safeguards" class="rs-secondary">See the safeguards <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div>
        </div>
        <div class="rs-observatory" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="rs-observatory__hex"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__ring rs-observatory__ring--outside"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__ring rs-observatory__ring--middle"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__ring rs-observatory__ring--inside"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__sweep"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__hub"><span>◆</span></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__ray rs-observatory__ray--left"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__ray rs-observatory__ray--right"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__pulse rs-observatory__pulse--a"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__pulse rs-observatory__pulse--b"></div>
          <div class="rs-observatory__pulse rs-observatory__pulse--c"></div>
          <span class="rs-observatory__readout rs-observatory__readout--top">POTENTIAL HARM</span>
          <span class="rs-observatory__readout rs-observatory__readout--bottom">EVIDENCE / RESPONSE</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rs-global" aria-labelledby="rs-global-title">
      <div class="rs-section-heading">
        <p class="rs-eyebrow rs-eyebrow--soft">WHY THE GLOBAL PICTURE MATTERS</p>
        <h2 id="rs-global-title">The U.S. isn't building AI alone. Why does leadership matter?</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-global__layout">
        <p class="rs-global__lead">Countries around the world are developing increasingly capable AI. Having the skills to build and understand these systems can help a country spot threats, strengthen its defenses, and influence the rules. That includes the United States — but it doesn't make any country automatically safe.</p>
        <div class="rs-global__split">
          <article><span class="rs-global__number">01 / CAPABILITY</span><h3>Knowing how it works helps us defend against it.</h3><p>Researchers and security teams can use AI expertise to test weaknesses and build tools that protect people and infrastructure.</p></article>
          <article><span class="rs-global__number">02 / RESPONSIBILITY</span><h3>Racing ahead without checks can create new risks.</h3><p>Competition may encourage faster releases. Testing, accountability, and cooperation with other countries still matter.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="rs-global__note">The goal isn't just being first. It's being capable <em>and</em> responsible. <a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST's risk framework</a> and the <a href="https://internationalaisafetyreport.org/publication/2026-report-executive-summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2026 international safety assessment</a> explain why both matter.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rs-jobs" aria-labelledby="rs-jobs-title">
      <div class="rs-jobs__heading">
        <div>
          <p class="rs-eyebrow rs-eyebrow--soft">FEATURED RISK <span class="rs-divide">/</span> WORK & ECONOMY</p>
          <h2 id="rs-jobs-title">Will AI take our jobs?</h2>
          <p>For some people, the first effect may not be getting laid off. It may be a job opening that never appears. We need to watch both.</p>
        </div>
        <span class="rs-jobs__tag"><span></span> HIGH · PROVISIONAL</span>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-jobs__metrics">
        <article class="rs-jobs__metric rs-jobs__metric--forecast">
          <span class="rs-jobs__eyebrow">AN INDUSTRY FORECAST / MAY 2025</span>
          <strong>Up to 50%</strong>
          <h3>Entry-level white-collar jobs</h3>
          <p>Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei warned that AI <em>could</em> eliminate up to half of these jobs within one to five years, with unemployment potentially reaching 10–20%.</p>
          <span class="rs-jobs__qualifier">A prediction, not an observed job-loss rate.</span>
          <a href="https://www.axios.com/2025/05/28/ai-jobs-white-collar-unemployment-anthropic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the 2025 interview <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </article>
        <article class="rs-jobs__metric rs-jobs__metric--observed">
          <span class="rs-jobs__eyebrow">EMPLOYMENT DATA / AUGUST 2026</span>
          <strong>19% gap</strong>
          <h3>Young workers in exposed jobs</h3>
          <p>In Stanford's payroll-data study, employment among 22–25-year-olds in highly AI-exposed occupations was 19% below the level implied by growth in less-exposed occupations.</p>
          <span class="rs-jobs__qualifier">Mostly weaker hiring; not proof AI caused a 19% job loss.</span>
          <a href="https://digitaleconomy.stanford.edu/publication/canaries-in-the-coal-mine-six-facts-about-the-recent-employment-effects-of-artificial-intelligence/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the Stanford analysis <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </article>
        <article class="rs-jobs__metric rs-jobs__metric--counter">
          <span class="rs-jobs__eyebrow">ECONOMY-WIDE CHECK / OCTOBER 2026</span>
          <strong>No clear shift</strong>
          <h3>Not yet a broad collapse</h3>
          <p>Yale's Budget Lab found no clear evidence of large-scale AI-driven labor-market disruption. That doesn't rule out harm in particular jobs or what may happen next.</p>
          <span class="rs-jobs__qualifier">Different studies track different workers and outcomes.</span>
          <a href="https://budgetlab.yale.edu/research/tracking-impact-ai-labor-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the Yale analysis <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-jobs__context">
        <div><span class="rs-jobs__context-label">WHAT OTHER LEADERS SAY</span><p>Even AI leaders disagree. In May 2026, OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said an AI-driven “jobs apocalypse” was unlikely in the near term, despite earlier concern about entry-level work.</p><a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/openais-altman-says-ai-unlikely-lead-jobs-apocalypse-2026-05-26/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reuters · May 2026 <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
        <div><span class="rs-jobs__context-label">WHAT EXPOSURE MEANS</span><p>The International Labour Organization found that roughly one in four workers has some exposure to generative AI. It expects most affected jobs to change rather than disappear entirely.</p><a href="https://www.ilo.org/publications/generative-ai-and-jobs-2025-update" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ILO · May 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      </div>
      <p class="rs-jobs__bottomline"><strong>The point:</strong> Fewer job openings, layoffs, changed responsibilities, and new opportunities are not the same thing. We should measure each separately — and take workers' concerns seriously before a crisis appears.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="risk-radar" class="rs-radar" aria-labelledby="rs-radar-title">
      <div class="rs-radar__heading">
        <div><p class="rs-eyebrow rs-eyebrow--soft">THE RISK RADAR <span class="rs-divide">/</span> 17 TOPICS</p><h2 id="rs-radar-title">Explore what could go wrong.</h2></div>
        <p>Choose a category, then select a risk for the fuller story. The levels describe <strong>potential harm</strong>, not the odds of something happening.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-legend" aria-labelledby="rs-legend-title">
        <div class="rs-legend__heading"><h3 id="rs-legend-title">What the levels mean</h3><span>Provisional editorial assessments · not a scientific risk score</span></div>
        <div class="rs-legend__grid">{riskLevels.map(level=>(<div class={"rs-legend__item rs-level--"+level.id}><strong>{level.name}</strong><p>{level.detail}</p></div>))}</div>
        <p class="rs-legend__foot">The color shows possible severity. The evidence label tells you whether harm is documented, capabilities are emerging, or the scenario remains uncertain. A “Critical” future scenario is not a confirmed event.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter risks by subject">
        <button type="button" data-rs-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All risks <span>{risks.length}</span></button>
        {riskGroups.map(group=><button type="button" data-rs-filter={group.id} aria-pressed="false">{group.title} <span>{risks.filter(r=>r.group===group.id).length}</span></button>)}
      </div>
      <p class="rs-filter-state" role="status" data-rs-count>Showing all {risks.length} risks</p>
      <div class="rs-grid">
        {risks.map((risk,i)=>(
          <button type="button" class={"rs-card rs-level--"+risk.level} data-rs-risk={risk.id} data-rs-group={risk.group}
            aria-controls={"rs-panel-"+risk.id} aria-pressed={i===0?"true":"false"}>
            <span class="rs-card__top"><span class="rs-card__category">{riskGroups.find(g=>g.id===risk.group)?.title}</span><span class="rs-card__level"><i></i>{risk.level}</span></span>
            <strong class="rs-card__title">{risk.name}</strong>
            <span class="rs-card__description">{risk.short}</span>
            <span class="rs-card__bottom"><span>EVIDENCE <b>{risk.evidence}</b></span><span class="rs-card__more">Explore <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span></span>
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="rs-stage" data-rs-stage>
        <div class="rs-stage__header"><span>INSIDE THE SIGNAL</span><span>WHAT IT MEANS / WHAT HELPS</span></div>
        {risks.map((risk,i)=>(
          <article id={"rs-panel-"+risk.id} data-rs-panel={risk.id} class={"rs-detail rs-level--"+risk.level}
            aria-labelledby={"rs-detail-title-"+risk.id} tabindex="-1" hidden={i>0}>
            <div class="rs-detail__main">
              <div class="rs-detail__meta"><span>{riskGroups.find(g=>g.id===risk.group)?.title}</span><span class="rs-detail__severity">{risk.level} · potential harm</span></div>
              <h3 id={"rs-detail-title-"+risk.id}>{risk.name}</h3>
              <p class="rs-detail__lead">{risk.longer}</p>
              <div class="rs-detail__facts">
                <div><span>EVIDENCE</span><strong>{risk.evidence}</strong></div>
                <div><span>DIRECTION</span><strong>{risk.trend}</strong></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="rs-detail__side">
              <div><h4>What the evidence doesn't tell us</h4><p>{risk.perspective}</p></div>
              <div><h4>What can help</h4><p>{risk.response}</p></div>
              <a href={risk.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{risk.sourceName} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <noscript><p class="rs-nojs">JavaScript is needed to switch the detailed panels. The list above still describes the risks, and the safeguards and source links below remain available.</p></noscript>
    </section>

    <section id="risk-safeguards" class="rs-safeguards" aria-labelledby="rs-safeguards-title">
      <div class="rs-safeguards__header">
        <p class="rs-eyebrow rs-eyebrow--soft">FROM SIGNAL TO SAFEGUARD</p>
        <h2 id="rs-safeguards-title">So what can we actually do?</h2>
        <p>No single company, government, or individual can solve every AI risk. Safety works best when multiple protections back each other up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-safeguards__grid">
        <article><span class="rs-safeguards__icon" aria-hidden="true">◇</span><span class="rs-safeguards__index">01 / COMPANIES & DEVELOPERS</span><h3>Build, test, and take responsibility.</h3><ul><li>Test dangerous capabilities and failure cases before release.</li><li>Limit what AI agents can access or change without approval.</li><li>Investigate incidents, fix problems, and publish meaningful findings.</li><li>Check how workplace automation affects employees and hiring.</li></ul></article>
        <article><span class="rs-safeguards__icon" aria-hidden="true">⌁</span><span class="rs-safeguards__index">02 / PUBLIC INSTITUTIONS</span><h3>Set rules people can trust.</h3><ul><li>Support independent evaluations and workable accountability.</li><li>Protect privacy, civil rights, and routes to challenge decisions.</li><li>Measure employment effects and help displaced workers transition.</li><li>Cooperate across borders on threats that don't stop at borders.</li></ul></article>
        <article><span class="rs-safeguards__icon" aria-hidden="true">✳</span><span class="rs-safeguards__index">03 / EVERYDAY PEOPLE</span><h3>Stay informed without living in fear.</h3><ul><li>Double-check surprising claims or urgent requests for money.</li><li>Keep personal and account information secure.</li><li>Don't rely on AI alone for high-stakes advice or decisions.</li><li>Learn how AI may change your work and ask for support early.</li></ul></article>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-safeguards__sources"><span>READ MORE ABOUT SAFETY PRACTICES</span><a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST AI Risk Management Framework <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="https://internationalaisafetyreport.org/publication/2026-report-executive-summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2026 International AI Safety Report <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="rs-eli5" aria-labelledby="rs-eli5-title">
      <div class="rs-eli5__inner">
        <p class="rs-eyebrow rs-eyebrow--soft">THE EVERYDAY EXPLANATION</p>
        <h2 id="rs-eli5-title" class="rs-eli5__title">
          <span class="rs-eli5__word rs-eli5__word--gold">Explain</span>
          <span class="rs-eli5__word rs-eli5__word--cyan">Like</span>
          <span class="rs-eli5__word rs-eli5__word--pink">I'm</span>
          <span class="rs-eli5__word rs-eli5__word--mint">Five:<svg class="rs-eli5__scribble" viewBox="0 0 163 22" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 11 Q37 2 77 9 T159 7"/><path d="M14 18 Q56 13 93 17 T153 15"/></svg></span>
        </h2>
        <p class="rs-eli5__lead">Four simple ideas to take away.</p>
        <div class="rs-eli5__grid">
          <article><span>01 / MISTAKES</span><h3>AI can get things wrong.</h3><p>Even when it sounds confident, AI can make mistakes. It's okay to check its work.</p></article>
          <article><span>02 / MISUSE</span><h3>People can use AI to trick others.</h3><p>A fake voice, picture, or message can seem real. Before trusting it, make sure it's genuine.</p></article>
          <article><span>03 / JOBS</span><h3>AI can change work.</h3><p>It may help with some tasks and replace others. People need time and support to adapt.</p></article>
          <article><span>04 / SAFETY</span><h3>Powerful tools need good rules.</h3><p>Testing, limits, and people paying attention help make AI safer.</p></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <footer class="rs-foot"><span>AI IN PERSPECTIVE · EDUCATIONAL RISK OVERVIEW · RESEARCH REVIEW PENDING</span><a href="/methodology">Our approach to evidence <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="/discover/">← All perspectives</a></footer>
  </main>
  <script>import "../../scripts/risks.js";</script>
</BaseLayout>
